[{"input":"{mainmatter=\"start\"}\n# Lecture 1 – Computing Concepts\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n\n- Define what a computer is.\n- Distinguish between hardware and software.\n- Explain the input-process-output-storage model.\n- Differentiate between general-purpose computers and embedded systems.\n- Explain the concept of abstraction in computing.\n- Describe the major milestones in the evolution of computing.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h1 id=\"md-12281wm-368-k1olys-lecture-1-computing-concepts\" mainmatter=\"start\">Lecture 1 – Computing Concepts</h1>\n<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-368-k1olys-learning-objectives\">Learning Objectives</h2>\n<p>At the end of this chapter, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Define what a computer is.\n</li>\n<li>Distinguish between hardware and software.\n</li>\n<li>Explain the input-process-output-storage model.\n</li>\n<li>Differentiate between general-purpose computers and embedded systems.\n</li>\n<li>Explain the concept of abstraction in computing.\n</li>\n<li>Describe the major milestones in the evolution of computing.\n</li>\n</ul>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Introduction\n\nComputers are among the most important technologies of modern society.\n\nThey are used in:\n\n- Communication\n- Transportation\n- Healthcare\n- Education\n- Entertainment\n- Scientific research\n- Business and finance\n\nWhen most people hear the word *computer*, they think of a laptop or desktop machine.\n\nHowever, computers exist in many forms:\n\n- Smartphones\n- Smartwatches\n- Cars\n- ATMs\n- Game consoles\n- Smart TVs\n- Home appliances\n- Industrial robots\n\nIn fact, a typical student may interact with dozens of computers every day without realizing it.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-369-19dqqe5-introduction\">Introduction</h2>\n<p>Computers are among the most important technologies of modern society.</p>\n<p>They are used in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Communication\n</li>\n<li>Transportation\n</li>\n<li>Healthcare\n</li>\n<li>Education\n</li>\n<li>Entertainment\n</li>\n<li>Scientific research\n</li>\n<li>Business and finance\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When most people hear the word <em>computer</em>, they think of a laptop or desktop machine.</p>\n<p>However, computers exist in many forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smartphones\n</li>\n<li>Smartwatches\n</li>\n<li>Cars\n</li>\n<li>ATMs\n</li>\n<li>Game consoles\n</li>\n<li>Smart TVs\n</li>\n<li>Home appliances\n</li>\n<li>Industrial robots\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In fact, a typical student may interact with dozens of computers every day without realizing it.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## What Is a Computer?\n\n### Definition\n\nA computer is a programmable machine that receives data, processes it according to instructions, produces results, and often stores information for future use.\n\nA simplified model is:\n\n```text\nInput → Processing → Output\n            ↑↓\n         Storage\n```\n\nAll computers, regardless of shape or size, perform these four basic functions.\n\n### Input\n\nInput is information entering the computer.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Typing on a keyboard\n- Clicking a mouse\n- Touching a smartphone screen\n- Speaking into a microphone\n- Reading data from a sensor\n\n### Processing\n\nProcessing is the transformation of input into useful information.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Calculating a result\n- Editing an image\n- Searching a database\n- Finding a route on a map\n\n### Output\n\nOutput is information produced by the computer.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Displaying text\n- Playing music\n- Printing a document\n- Showing a web page\n\n### Storage\n\nStorage allows information to be retained.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Documents\n- Photos\n- Videos\n- Installed applications\n\nStorage enables computers to remember information after they are powered off.\n\n### Example: Online Navigation\n\nSuppose you request directions to Brussels.\n\n#### Input\n\n- Current location\n- Destination\n\n#### Processing\n\n- Route calculation\n- Traffic analysis\n\n#### Output\n\n- Directions\n- Travel time\n- Map display\n\n#### Storage\n\n- Saved locations\n- Route history\n\nThis simple example demonstrates all four functions of a computer.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-what-is-a-computer\">What Is a Computer?</h2>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-definition\">Definition</h3>\n<p>A computer is a programmable machine that receives data, processes it according to instructions, produces results, and often stores information for future use.</p>\n<p>A simplified model is:</p>\n<pre class=\"markdown-code-block\"><code>Input → Processing → Output\n            ↑↓\n         Storage</code></pre>\n<p>All computers, regardless of shape or size, perform these four basic functions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-input\">Input</h3>\n<p>Input is information entering the computer.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typing on a keyboard\n</li>\n<li>Clicking a mouse\n</li>\n<li>Touching a smartphone screen\n</li>\n<li>Speaking into a microphone\n</li>\n<li>Reading data from a sensor\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-processing\">Processing</h3>\n<p>Processing is the transformation of input into useful information.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calculating a result\n</li>\n<li>Editing an image\n</li>\n<li>Searching a database\n</li>\n<li>Finding a route on a map\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-output\">Output</h3>\n<p>Output is information produced by the computer.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Displaying text\n</li>\n<li>Playing music\n</li>\n<li>Printing a document\n</li>\n<li>Showing a web page\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-storage\">Storage</h3>\n<p>Storage allows information to be retained.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Documents\n</li>\n<li>Photos\n</li>\n<li>Videos\n</li>\n<li>Installed applications\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Storage enables computers to remember information after they are powered off.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-example-online-navigation\">Example: Online Navigation</h3>\n<p>Suppose you request directions to Brussels.</p>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-input-1\">Input</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Current location\n</li>\n<li>Destination\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-processing-1\">Processing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Route calculation\n</li>\n<li>Traffic analysis\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-output-1\">Output</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Directions\n</li>\n<li>Travel time\n</li>\n<li>Map display\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-370-16qbq9f-storage-1\">Storage</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Saved locations\n</li>\n<li>Route history\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This simple example demonstrates all four functions of a computer.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Hardware and Software\n\nA computer system consists of two major elements:\n\n```text\nHardware\n+\nSoftware\n=\nComputer System\n```\n\n### Hardware\n\nHardware refers to the physical components of a computer.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Processor\n- Memory\n- Storage devices\n- Display\n- Keyboard\n- Network card\n\nHardware can be touched and physically examined.\n\n### Software\n\nSoftware consists of instructions that tell hardware what to do.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Microsoft Word\n- Google Chrome\n- Mobile applications\n\nSoftware cannot be touched physically. It exists as digital information stored inside computer systems.\n\n### Analogy\n\nConsider a music player.\n\n#### Hardware\n\n- Speakers\n- Buttons\n- Battery\n- Screen\n\n#### Software\n\n- The program that plays music\n\nWithout software, the hardware does nothing useful. Without hardware, the software cannot run.\n\n### Important Observation\n\nThe same hardware can perform very different tasks simply by changing the software.\n\nFor example, the same smartphone can be used for:\n\n- Navigation\n- Banking\n- Gaming\n- Messaging\n- Photography\n\nThe hardware remains largely unchanged. The software determines the behavior.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-371-gt9fnz-hardware-and-software\">Hardware and Software</h2>\n<p>A computer system consists of two major elements:</p>\n<pre class=\"markdown-code-block\"><code>Hardware\n+\nSoftware\n=\nComputer System</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-371-gt9fnz-hardware\">Hardware</h3>\n<p>Hardware refers to the physical components of a computer.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Processor\n</li>\n<li>Memory\n</li>\n<li>Storage devices\n</li>\n<li>Display\n</li>\n<li>Keyboard\n</li>\n<li>Network card\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hardware can be touched and physically examined.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-371-gt9fnz-software\">Software</h3>\n<p>Software consists of instructions that tell hardware what to do.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows\n</li>\n<li>Linux\n</li>\n<li>macOS\n</li>\n<li>Microsoft Word\n</li>\n<li>Google Chrome\n</li>\n<li>Mobile applications\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Software cannot be touched physically. It exists as digital information stored inside computer systems.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-371-gt9fnz-analogy\">Analogy</h3>\n<p>Consider a music player.</p>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-371-gt9fnz-hardware-1\">Hardware</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Speakers\n</li>\n<li>Buttons\n</li>\n<li>Battery\n</li>\n<li>Screen\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-371-gt9fnz-software-1\">Software</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The program that plays music\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without software, the hardware does nothing useful. Without hardware, the software cannot run.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-371-gt9fnz-important-observation\">Important Observation</h3>\n<p>The same hardware can perform very different tasks simply by changing the software.</p>\n<p>For example, the same smartphone can be used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Navigation\n</li>\n<li>Banking\n</li>\n<li>Gaming\n</li>\n<li>Messaging\n</li>\n<li>Photography\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hardware remains largely unchanged. The software determines the behavior.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## General-Purpose and Embedded Systems\n\nNot all computers are designed for the same purpose.\n\n### General-Purpose Computers\n\nGeneral-purpose computers can perform many different tasks.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Laptops\n- Desktop PCs\n- Smartphones\n- Tablets\n\nUsers can install and remove software as needed.\n\n### Embedded Systems\n\nAn embedded system is a computer designed for a specific purpose.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Microwave oven controller\n- Washing machine controller\n- Traffic lights\n- Card payment terminals\n- Smart thermostats\n\nThese computers focus on a limited set of tasks.\n\n### Comparison\n\n#### General-Purpose Computer\n\n- Many applications\n- Flexible\n- User-controlled\n\nExample: Laptop\n\n#### Embedded System\n\n- Specific purpose\n- Fixed functionality\n- Often invisible to users\n\nExample: Digital thermostat","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-372-1oiq8m0-general-purpose-and-embedded-systems\">General-Purpose and Embedded Systems</h2>\n<p>Not all computers are designed for the same purpose.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-372-1oiq8m0-general-purpose-computers\">General-Purpose Computers</h3>\n<p>General-purpose computers can perform many different tasks.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laptops\n</li>\n<li>Desktop PCs\n</li>\n<li>Smartphones\n</li>\n<li>Tablets\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Users can install and remove software as needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-372-1oiq8m0-embedded-systems\">Embedded Systems</h3>\n<p>An embedded system is a computer designed for a specific purpose.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microwave oven controller\n</li>\n<li>Washing machine controller\n</li>\n<li>Traffic lights\n</li>\n<li>Card payment terminals\n</li>\n<li>Smart thermostats\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These computers focus on a limited set of tasks.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-372-1oiq8m0-comparison\">Comparison</h3>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-372-1oiq8m0-general-purpose-computer\">General-Purpose Computer</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Many applications\n</li>\n<li>Flexible\n</li>\n<li>User-controlled\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: Laptop</p>\n<h4 id=\"md-12281wm-372-1oiq8m0-embedded-system\">Embedded System</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Specific purpose\n</li>\n<li>Fixed functionality\n</li>\n<li>Often invisible to users\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: Digital thermostat</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Levels of Abstraction\n\n### Why Abstraction Is Necessary\n\nModern computers are extremely complex. No single person understands every detail simultaneously.\n\nAbstraction allows us to manage complexity by organizing systems into layers. Each layer hides details from the layers above it.\n\n### Everyday Example: Driving a Car\n\nMost drivers know:\n\n- Accelerator\n- Brake\n- Steering wheel\n\nThey do not need to understand:\n\n- Combustion\n- Fuel injection\n- Gearbox mechanics\n\nThe car hides complexity. This is abstraction.\n\n### Abstraction in Computing\n\nWhen writing a document:\n\n```text\nUser\n↓\nWord Processor\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n↓\nElectronics\n↓\nPhysics\n```\n\nMost users only interact with the top layer. The lower layers work automatically.\n\n### Why Abstraction Matters\n\nWithout abstraction:\n\n- Software development would be extremely difficult.\n- Modern systems would be impossible to manage.\n- Innovation would be much slower.\n\nAbstraction allows specialists to focus on a particular layer.\n\n### Example\n\nA website developer does not need to understand:\n\n- Semiconductor fabrication\n- Electrical engineering\n- Processor design\n\nThe lower layers are hidden through abstraction.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-373-1hjl1q-levels-of-abstraction\">Levels of Abstraction</h2>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-373-1hjl1q-why-abstraction-is-necessary\">Why Abstraction Is Necessary</h3>\n<p>Modern computers are extremely complex. No single person understands every detail simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Abstraction allows us to manage complexity by organizing systems into layers. Each layer hides details from the layers above it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-373-1hjl1q-everyday-example-driving-a-car\">Everyday Example: Driving a Car</h3>\n<p>Most drivers know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Accelerator\n</li>\n<li>Brake\n</li>\n<li>Steering wheel\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They do not need to understand:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Combustion\n</li>\n<li>Fuel injection\n</li>\n<li>Gearbox mechanics\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The car hides complexity. This is abstraction.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-373-1hjl1q-abstraction-in-computing\">Abstraction in Computing</h3>\n<p>When writing a document:</p>\n<pre class=\"markdown-code-block\"><code>User\n↓\nWord Processor\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n↓\nElectronics\n↓\nPhysics</code></pre>\n<p>Most users only interact with the top layer. The lower layers work automatically.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-373-1hjl1q-why-abstraction-matters\">Why Abstraction Matters</h3>\n<p>Without abstraction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Software development would be extremely difficult.\n</li>\n<li>Modern systems would be impossible to manage.\n</li>\n<li>Innovation would be much slower.\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Abstraction allows specialists to focus on a particular layer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-373-1hjl1q-example\">Example</h3>\n<p>A website developer does not need to understand:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Semiconductor fabrication\n</li>\n<li>Electrical engineering\n</li>\n<li>Processor design\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lower layers are hidden through abstraction.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The Computing Stack\n\nA useful view of a computer system is the computing stack.\n\n```text\nUsers\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n↓\nElectronics\n↓\nPhysical Phenomena\n```\n\n### Users\n\nHumans who interact with the system.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Students\n- Employees\n- Customers\n\n### Applications\n\nPrograms performing specific tasks.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Word processors\n- Browsers\n- Games\n\n### Operating System\n\nThe software layer managing the hardware and providing services to applications.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- Android\n\n### Hardware\n\nPhysical computing components.\n\nExamples:\n\n- CPU\n- Memory\n- Storage\n\n### Electronics\n\nElectrical circuits that enable digital computation.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-374-19m3akm-the-computing-stack\">The Computing Stack</h2>\n<p>A useful view of a computer system is the computing stack.</p>\n<pre class=\"markdown-code-block\"><code>Users\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n↓\nElectronics\n↓\nPhysical Phenomena</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-374-19m3akm-users\">Users</h3>\n<p>Humans who interact with the system.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Students\n</li>\n<li>Employees\n</li>\n<li>Customers\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-374-19m3akm-applications\">Applications</h3>\n<p>Programs performing specific tasks.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Word processors\n</li>\n<li>Browsers\n</li>\n<li>Games\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-374-19m3akm-operating-system\">Operating System</h3>\n<p>The software layer managing the hardware and providing services to applications.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows\n</li>\n<li>Linux\n</li>\n<li>Android\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-374-19m3akm-hardware\">Hardware</h3>\n<p>Physical computing components.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CPU\n</li>\n<li>Memory\n</li>\n<li>Storage\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-374-19m3akm-electronics\">Electronics</h3>\n<p>Electrical circuits that enable digital computation.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## A Brief History of Computing\n\n### Early Computing Tools\n\nHumans have always needed help performing calculations.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Counting sticks\n- Abacus\n- Mechanical calculators\n\nThese devices assisted with arithmetic but had limited capabilities.\n\n### Mechanical Computing\n\nIn the nineteenth century, engineers envisioned machines capable of performing complex calculations automatically.\n\nThe key idea emerged: A machine could follow a sequence of instructions.\n\nThis concept remains central to computing today.\n\n### Electronic Computing\n\nIn the twentieth century, electronics transformed computation.\n\nBenefits included:\n\n- Higher speed\n- Better reliability\n- Increased automation\n\nEarly computers occupied entire rooms.\n\n### Personal Computing\n\nThe introduction of affordable personal computers made computing available to individuals and small organizations.\n\nPersonal computers enabled:\n\n- Word processing\n- Spreadsheets\n- Electronic communication\n\n### Internet Era\n\nThe Internet connected computers worldwide.\n\nNew possibilities emerged:\n\n- Email\n- Web browsing\n- Online commerce\n- Cloud services\n\n### Mobile Computing\n\nComputing moved into pockets.\n\nModern smartphones combine:\n\n- Communication\n- Navigation\n- Photography\n- Entertainment\n- Internet access\n\nin a single device.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-375-1tjm0sx-a-brief-history-of-computing\">A Brief History of Computing</h2>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-375-1tjm0sx-early-computing-tools\">Early Computing Tools</h3>\n<p>Humans have always needed help performing calculations.</p>\n<p>Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Counting sticks\n</li>\n<li>Abacus\n</li>\n<li>Mechanical calculators\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These devices assisted with arithmetic but had limited capabilities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-375-1tjm0sx-mechanical-computing\">Mechanical Computing</h3>\n<p>In the nineteenth century, engineers envisioned machines capable of performing complex calculations automatically.</p>\n<p>The key idea emerged: A machine could follow a sequence of instructions.</p>\n<p>This concept remains central to computing today.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-375-1tjm0sx-electronic-computing\">Electronic Computing</h3>\n<p>In the twentieth century, electronics transformed computation.</p>\n<p>Benefits included:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Higher speed\n</li>\n<li>Better reliability\n</li>\n<li>Increased automation\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Early computers occupied entire rooms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-375-1tjm0sx-personal-computing\">Personal Computing</h3>\n<p>The introduction of affordable personal computers made computing available to individuals and small organizations.</p>\n<p>Personal computers enabled:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Word processing\n</li>\n<li>Spreadsheets\n</li>\n<li>Electronic communication\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-375-1tjm0sx-internet-era\">Internet Era</h3>\n<p>The Internet connected computers worldwide.</p>\n<p>New possibilities emerged:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Email\n</li>\n<li>Web browsing\n</li>\n<li>Online commerce\n</li>\n<li>Cloud services\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-375-1tjm0sx-mobile-computing\">Mobile Computing</h3>\n<p>Computing moved into pockets.</p>\n<p>Modern smartphones combine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Communication\n</li>\n<li>Navigation\n</li>\n<li>Photography\n</li>\n<li>Entertainment\n</li>\n<li>Internet access\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>in a single device.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The Digital Transformation\n\nComputers have changed nearly every sector.\n\n### Communication\n\n- Email\n- Messaging\n- Video conferencing\n\n### Education\n\n- Learning platforms\n- Digital libraries\n- Online courses\n\n### Business\n\n- E-commerce\n- Inventory systems\n- Data analytics\n\n### Healthcare\n\n- Medical imaging\n- Electronic patient records\n- Diagnostic support systems\n\n### Entertainment\n\n- Streaming services\n- Gaming\n- Social media\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Increased productivity\n- Faster communication\n- Global access to information\n\n### Challenges\n\n- Security threats\n- Privacy concerns\n- Dependence on technology\n- Information overload\n\nSeveral of these topics will be revisited later in the course.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-the-digital-transformation\">The Digital Transformation</h2>\n<p>Computers have changed nearly every sector.</p>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-communication\">Communication</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Email\n</li>\n<li>Messaging\n</li>\n<li>Video conferencing\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-education\">Education</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Learning platforms\n</li>\n<li>Digital libraries\n</li>\n<li>Online courses\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-business\">Business</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>E-commerce\n</li>\n<li>Inventory systems\n</li>\n<li>Data analytics\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-healthcare\">Healthcare</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Medical imaging\n</li>\n<li>Electronic patient records\n</li>\n<li>Diagnostic support systems\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-entertainment\">Entertainment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Streaming services\n</li>\n<li>Gaming\n</li>\n<li>Social media\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-benefits\">Benefits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased productivity\n</li>\n<li>Faster communication\n</li>\n<li>Global access to information\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"md-12281wm-376-l0w4t1-challenges\">Challenges</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Security threats\n</li>\n<li>Privacy concerns\n</li>\n<li>Dependence on technology\n</li>\n<li>Information overload\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Several of these topics will be revisited later in the course.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Summary\n\nA computer is a programmable machine that performs four fundamental functions:\n\n- Input\n- Processing\n- Output\n- Storage\n\nKey concepts from this chapter:\n\n- Computers are everywhere.\n- Hardware consists of physical components.\n- Software consists of instructions and programs.\n- General-purpose computers can perform many tasks.\n- Embedded systems are designed for specific tasks.\n- Abstraction allows complex systems to be understood through layers.\n- The computing stack links users to hardware.\n- Computing has evolved from simple mechanical tools to globally connected digital systems.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-377-1rfe4gu-summary\">Summary</h2>\n<p>A computer is a programmable machine that performs four fundamental functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Input\n</li>\n<li>Processing\n</li>\n<li>Output\n</li>\n<li>Storage\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Key concepts from this chapter:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Computers are everywhere.\n</li>\n<li>Hardware consists of physical components.\n</li>\n<li>Software consists of instructions and programs.\n</li>\n<li>General-purpose computers can perform many tasks.\n</li>\n<li>Embedded systems are designed for specific tasks.\n</li>\n<li>Abstraction allows complex systems to be understood through layers.\n</li>\n<li>The computing stack links users to hardware.\n</li>\n<li>Computing has evolved from simple mechanical tools to globally connected digital systems.\n</li>\n</ul>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Key Terms\n\n| Term             | Definition                                      |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| Computer         | Programmable machine that processes data        |\n| Hardware         | Physical components of a computer               |\n| Software         | Programs and instructions executed by hardware  |\n| Input            | Information entering a system                   |\n| Processing       | Transformation of data                          |\n| Output           | Information produced by a system                |\n| Storage          | Retention of information                        |\n| Embedded System  | Computer dedicated to a specific task           |\n| Abstraction      | Hiding complexity through layers                |\n| Operating System | Software that manages hardware and applications |","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-378-2i5juw-key-terms\">Key Terms</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Computer</td><td>Programmable machine that processes data</td></tr><tr><td>Hardware</td><td>Physical components of a computer</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Programs and instructions executed by hardware</td></tr><tr><td>Input</td><td>Information entering a system</td></tr><tr><td>Processing</td><td>Transformation of data</td></tr><tr><td>Output</td><td>Information produced by a system</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Retention of information</td></tr><tr><td>Embedded System</td><td>Computer dedicated to a specific task</td></tr><tr><td>Abstraction</td><td>Hiding complexity through layers</td></tr><tr><td>Operating System</td><td>Software that manages hardware and applications</td></tr></tbody></table>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Review Questions\n\n- What are the four fundamental functions of a computer?\n- What is the difference between hardware and software?\n- Give three examples of embedded systems.\n- Why are modern computers dependent on abstraction?\n- What is the role of an operating system?\n- How does a smartphone differ from an embedded system?\n- Describe the layers of the computing stack.\n- Why was the Internet an important milestone in the history of computing?\n- Give two benefits and two challenges of the digital transformation.\n- Explain why a computer can perform many different tasks even if its hardware remains unchanged.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-379-1o1x3rt-review-questions\">Review Questions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the four fundamental functions of a computer?\n</li>\n<li>What is the difference between hardware and software?\n</li>\n<li>Give three examples of embedded systems.\n</li>\n<li>Why are modern computers dependent on abstraction?\n</li>\n<li>What is the role of an operating system?\n</li>\n<li>How does a smartphone differ from an embedded system?\n</li>\n<li>Describe the layers of the computing stack.\n</li>\n<li>Why was the Internet an important milestone in the history of computing?\n</li>\n<li>Give two benefits and two challenges of the digital transformation.\n</li>\n<li>Explain why a computer can perform many different tasks even if its hardware remains unchanged.\n</li>\n</ul>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Transition to Lecture 2\n\nWe now understand **what a computer is** and **how computing systems are organized**. The next question is:\n\nHow does a computer represent information?\n\nIn Lecture 2, we will discover how text, images, sound, and video are all transformed into binary data composed of bits and bytes.","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-12281wm-380-41srt0-transition-to-lecture-2\">Transition to Lecture 2</h2>\n<p>We now understand <strong>what a computer is</strong> and <strong>how computing systems are organized</strong>. The next question is:</p>\n<p>How does a computer represent information?</p>\n<p>In Lecture 2, we will discover how text, images, sound, and video are all transformed into binary data composed of bits and bytes.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]